  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
